1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:06,468
This might genuinely be one of the biggest breakthroughs in medicine and drug discovery we've seen in decades.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:11,520
A new paper just dropped, and it's about this new AI model called MAML.

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:15,200
And this can completely transform biosciences and medicine.

4
00:00:15,374 --> 00:00:23,876
It could potentially come up with a ton of new cures for diseases like cancer, and this can make drug discovery way faster and cheaper and more accurate.

5
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,428
This could also lead to personalized medicine and beyond.

6
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:36,516
Now, this paper is super technical and jam-packed with details, so in this video I'm going to break it down into simple terms so it's easy for anyone to understand.

7
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:37,920
Let's jump right in.

8
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:45,636
Now to understand why this paper is such a big deal, it's helpful to first start with the problems of medicine and drug design today.

9
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:53,280
Imagine spending 10 years and a billion dollars building something, and there's a 90% chance it just doesn't work.

10
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:54,000
90%!

11
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,644
That's the failure rate of new drugs.

12
00:00:56,942 --> 00:01:05,280
From the moment scientists come up with a promising new medicine to the moment it goes through clinical trials, roughly 90% don't make it through approval.

13
00:01:05,454 --> 00:01:07,492
That's kind of insane when you think about it.

14
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:22,560
We've already sequenced the human genome, we have smartphones, we have AI that can predict protein structures or even understand DNA, we have so much amazing technology, yet when it comes to actually making new medicine, we're still wrong most of the time.

15
00:01:22,894 --> 00:01:23,556
Think about this.

16
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:28,240
If I built bridges and 9 out of 10 of them collapsed, that would be horrible.

17
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:29,412
I would be out of business.

18
00:01:29,742 --> 00:01:35,440
So why is modern medicine, even with all our insane modern technology, still hitting this wall?

19
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,960
To understand this, we first need to understand some basic biology and how drug design works.

20
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:43,492
You see, every living thing has DNA.

21
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:47,652
This is the code for living things to function and stay alive.

22
00:01:47,918 --> 00:01:50,628
And within DNA, there are sections called genes.

23
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:52,900
These are basically instruction manuals.

24
00:01:53,230 --> 00:02:00,400
They contain the code for making proteins, and proteins are the tiny machines that actually do most of the work inside your body.

25
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:08,452
They send signals, build structures, speed up chemical reactions, control immune responses, keep cells alive, and a ton of other stuff.

26
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:10,720
But genes aren't always active.

27
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:14,560
Some genes can be turned up, some turned down or mutated.

28
00:02:14,734 --> 00:02:21,440
Scientists call this gene expression, basically a measurement of which genes are active and how active they are.

29
00:02:21,678 --> 00:02:24,000
And this controls how many proteins are made.

30
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:31,652
So we have DNA which contains genes, which code up proteins, which are essential for the function of all living things.

31
00:02:32,046 --> 00:02:37,092
I'm oversimplifying here, but that's essentially the mini lesson on how life works.

32
00:02:37,422 --> 00:02:42,212
Now there are a few possible ways for this pathway to go wrong, which could cause disease.

33
00:02:42,478 --> 00:02:45,796
The DNA might get mutations, which would mess things up.

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,508
This could cause proteins to be made incorrectly or not at all.

35
00:02:49,710 --> 00:02:54,948
Or the gene expression might be too high or too low, which also affects how proteins are made.

36
00:02:55,120 --> 00:03:01,604
Even subtle errors in this pathway could cause some catastrophic diseases that you and I are familiar with, like cancer.

37
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:10,564
In fact, in really simple terms, how cancer works is there might be a mutation that damages a gene in the DNA, which controls for cell division.

38
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,920
This changes gene expression or protein function.

39
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:20,452
And subsequently, these proteins then tell the cell to grow, to ignore stop signals, and spread into a tumor.

40
00:03:20,622 --> 00:03:21,828
That's essentially cancer.

41
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:25,892
Now, here's how drugs are currently designed around this process.

42
00:03:26,254 --> 00:03:32,160
Scientists usually start by trying to find the specific part of the disease pathway that has gone wrong.

43
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:34,500
In other words, they look for the bad guy.

44
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:37,920
Maybe there's a protein selling cancer cells to keep dividing.

45
00:03:38,094 --> 00:03:40,800
Maybe there's an enzyme helping a virus copy itself.

46
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:47,040
Anyways, once the researchers find that target, the goal is to design a drug that can interact with it.

47
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:50,880
Think of the target like a lock and the drug like a key.

48
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:55,908
If the key fits within the lock, it can either block the protein or change what it does.

49
00:03:56,174 --> 00:04:00,228
Now there are two main types of drugs that are relevant to this paper.

50
00:04:00,526 --> 00:04:03,652
Some drugs are small molecules like Tylenol.

51
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:07,748
They're tiny, stable, and often easy to manufacture as pills.

52
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,680
And because they're so small, they can sometimes just slip inside cells and bind to proteins there.

53
00:04:14,062 --> 00:04:17,792
Another type of drug is called an antibody, which is much larger.

54
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:21,380
You can think of them as highly precise biological clamps.

55
00:04:21,712 --> 00:04:25,472
They're designed to stick to the bad guy with extreme accuracy.

56
00:04:25,646 --> 00:04:30,404
Because of their size, they usually work outside cells or on the surface of cells.

57
00:04:30,672 --> 00:04:36,708
The hope is that if you shut down the bad guy, the disease such as cancer would also stop growing and die.

58
00:04:37,038 --> 00:04:42,752
But this is where it gets incredibly difficult, because the body is not just one simple machine.

59
00:04:42,926 --> 00:04:45,028
It's made of a billion moving parts.

60
00:04:45,326 --> 00:04:52,836
Even though the designed drug could successfully bind to the bad guy, it might affect other parts of the body as well and have some side effects.

61
00:04:53,326 --> 00:04:56,832
So it's not as simple as the analogy of a key and a lock.

62
00:04:56,992 --> 00:05:02,084
Maybe that key can also open other locks in the body which could cause harmful effects.

63
00:05:02,478 --> 00:05:07,204
So drug design is basically an attempt to find the perfect molecular tool.

64
00:05:07,534 --> 00:05:15,748
Strong enough to stop the disease, but precise enough so that it doesn't also harm the rest of the body, and safe enough to give to a real human.

65
00:05:16,014 --> 00:05:21,572
You also have to prove the drug actually works at safe doses and it doesn't accidentally kill the patient.

66
00:05:21,872 --> 00:05:24,192
That's the real challenge of drug design.

67
00:05:24,398 --> 00:05:27,972
And this is where modern technology runs into a weird problem.

68
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:35,108
You see, we have incredibly powerful tools today, but most of them only understand one slice of the puzzle.

69
00:05:35,406 --> 00:05:41,284
One AI model might predict protein structures, in fact, that's exactly what Google's Alpha Fold does.

70
00:05:41,552 --> 00:05:44,752
Another AI might be great at reading and generating DNA.

71
00:05:44,912 --> 00:05:51,312
One of them is called Evo 2, and in fact I covered this in a previous video, so see this if you want to learn more.

72
00:05:51,534 --> 00:05:58,352
And we have other tools that can screen chemical compounds, others that can analyze clinical trial data, and so on and so forth.

73
00:05:58,512 --> 00:06:02,112
But here's the thing disease does not happen in separate folders.

74
00:06:02,272 --> 00:06:09,828
It flows through the entire system, from DNA to gene activity, to proteins, to cells, and then to entire bodies.

75
00:06:10,126 --> 00:06:13,188
The problem is that all these tools are often siloed.

76
00:06:13,472 --> 00:06:18,340
They're trained on different datasets, built by different teams, optimized for different tasks.

77
00:06:18,702 --> 00:06:26,692
So instead of one system that understands the full chain of biology, we have a bunch of specialized tools looking at disconnected snapshots.

78
00:06:27,054 --> 00:06:37,472
It's like trying to solve a crime scene where one detective only has the fingerprints, another detective only has the security footage, another one only has the final autopsy report.

79
00:06:37,678 --> 00:06:43,392
Each clue matters, but they don't really talk to each other, so they can't connect everything into one story.

80
00:06:43,566 --> 00:06:46,272
Well that's exactly what MAML is trying to solve.

81
00:06:46,446 --> 00:06:50,340
They've built an AI model that understands everything at once.

82
00:06:50,672 --> 00:06:54,404
Chemistry, genetics, and protein structure simultaneously.

83
00:06:54,606 --> 00:06:57,872
And the scale of what they did is honestly insane.

84
00:06:58,254 --> 00:07:01,444
MAML was pre-trained on two billion samples.

85
00:07:01,646 --> 00:07:10,032
They scraped basically every major biological database out there: the observed antibody space, which contains billions of antibody sequences;

86
00:07:10,352 --> 00:07:13,712
Uniprot, which contains almost every protein we know;

87
00:07:13,966 --> 00:07:18,912
and then we also have Zinc and PubChem, which contains millions of small molecule structures;

88
00:07:19,246 --> 00:07:22,672
Cell X gene, which contains a ton of gene expression data.

89
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:27,364
So the model was trained on everything to do with chemistry, genetics, and proteins.

90
00:07:27,662 --> 00:07:30,340
But how does the AI actually read all of this?

91
00:07:30,606 --> 00:07:32,352
These are in different formats, right?

92
00:07:32,526 --> 00:07:38,788
A small molecule like aspirin looks nothing like a gene, which also looks nothing like an antibody.

93
00:07:39,214 --> 00:07:41,152
So the researchers did something clever.

94
00:07:41,392 --> 00:07:48,832
They forced everything into a single unified format sequences of characters, but each domain has its own grammar.

95
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:52,656
For molecules, they used something called a SMILE string.

96
00:07:52,976 --> 00:07:58,404
This is basically a way to flatten a 3D chemical structure into just one line of text.

97
00:07:58,736 --> 00:08:07,856
For example, the SMILE for Tylenol becomes this, where every letter is an atom and every symbol, like the equal sign, is a chemical bond.

98
00:08:08,078 --> 00:08:13,924
The AI learns chemistry just by tracking patterns across millions of these text strings.

99
00:08:14,414 --> 00:08:16,484
Now for genes, it's totally different.

100
00:08:16,718 --> 00:08:21,296
The model takes every gene in a cell and ranks them by how active they are.

101
00:08:21,536 --> 00:08:24,068
In other words, how much they are expressed.

102
00:08:24,334 --> 00:08:26,976
The genes screaming the loudest go first.

103
00:08:27,182 --> 00:08:28,644
The silent ones go last.

104
00:08:29,006 --> 00:08:34,308
So the model reads a cell as a priority list of which genes are doing the most work right now.

105
00:08:34,542 --> 00:08:40,420
And then for proteins and antibodies, which are also proteins, it just reads the chain of amino acids.

106
00:08:40,816 --> 00:08:42,660
These are the building blocks of proteins.

107
00:08:43,136 --> 00:08:45,796
But right now everything is in different formats.

108
00:08:46,126 --> 00:08:53,668
It's like you're reading a book, but the first paragraph is in English, the second paragraph is in sheet music, and the third paragraph is in JavaScript.

109
00:08:53,936 --> 00:08:55,456
It gets really confusing, right?

110
00:08:55,616 --> 00:08:57,056
This doesn't make sense at all.

111
00:08:57,294 --> 00:09:02,308
And if you throw all this raw data to train a neural network, it also gets super confused.

112
00:09:02,574 --> 00:09:05,216
Well, this is where the engineering gets super clever.

113
00:09:05,518 --> 00:09:08,416
MAML uses what's called a modular tokenizer.

114
00:09:08,590 --> 00:09:16,708
Think of a tokenizer as a dictionary the AI uses to translate this raw data into consistent language that the AI understands.

115
00:09:16,974 --> 00:09:19,456
But MAML doesn't just use one dictionary.

116
00:09:19,616 --> 00:09:24,388
It uses an umbrella tokenizer with specialized subdictionaries underneath.

117
00:09:24,654 --> 00:09:30,180
So there's one dictionary for chemistry, one dictionary for genetics, and another dictionary for proteins.

118
00:09:30,574 --> 00:09:37,444
If it sees a small molecule, it uses the small molecule dictionary to convert that into tokens and embeddings.

119
00:09:37,710 --> 00:09:42,576
If it gets a protein, it uses the protein dictionary to convert that into embeddings.

120
00:09:42,926 --> 00:09:44,708
And the same logic applies to genes.

121
00:09:45,038 --> 00:09:46,256
And here's the magic part.

122
00:09:46,496 --> 00:09:54,532
Once everything is translated and converted into these embeddings, they all get mixed together into a shared multidimensional space.

123
00:09:54,990 --> 00:10:01,188
So the model learns chemistry, proteins, and genetic expression all in one unified space.

124
00:10:01,486 --> 00:10:06,916
Because it merges all these domains together, it can learn the relationships between all these different things.

125
00:10:07,214 --> 00:10:11,696
Now this design sounds great, but how well does it actually perform in the real world?

126
00:10:11,886 --> 00:10:14,816
For example, can it actually predict drug behavior?

127
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:18,756
Well, the researchers put MAML through some pretty intense tests.

128
00:10:19,214 --> 00:10:28,496
Specifically, they evaluated it across 11 distinct, highly rigorous benchmarks that span the entire drug discovery pipeline.

129
00:10:28,656 --> 00:10:33,856
They threw everything at it, and it turns out the results across the board were phenomenal.

130
00:10:34,126 --> 00:10:40,388
So here's the table of results, and across these 11 benchmarks, MAML achieved state-of-the-art performance.

131
00:10:40,718 --> 00:10:45,700
It outright beat the previous best models in the world on all of these tasks.

132
00:10:46,096 --> 00:10:48,324
Let's dig into some specific benchmarks.

133
00:10:48,590 --> 00:10:52,496
Earlier we discussed the nightmare of designing a drug that actually works.

134
00:10:52,718 --> 00:10:56,816
It's able to reduce the disease, but it also ends up being toxic to the human.

135
00:10:57,070 --> 00:10:59,460
So is MAML able to predict these scenarios?

136
00:10:59,822 --> 00:11:04,292
They evaluated MAML on two critical safety benchmarks.

137
00:11:04,496 --> 00:11:09,296
One is called BBBP, which stands for blood brain barrier penetration.

138
00:11:09,616 --> 00:11:16,256
Another one is Clintox, which is a dataset used to predict clinical toxicity and FDA approval.

139
00:11:16,416 --> 00:11:19,536
Let's pause on this blood brain barrier for a second.

140
00:11:19,696 --> 00:11:22,336
This is actually a massive hurdle in pharmacology.

141
00:11:22,510 --> 00:11:25,856
You see, the brain has this incredibly tight security system.

142
00:11:26,126 --> 00:11:29,296
Of course, it doesn't want any external stuff flowing into the brain.

143
00:11:29,422 --> 00:11:38,016
But if you're designing a therapeutic for something like Alzheimer's or Parkinson's, your drug must be able to penetrate this barrier to reach the brain.

144
00:11:38,222 --> 00:11:47,936
Or conversely, if you're designing a really heavy chemotherapy drug for the liver, you absolutely do not want it crossing this barrier and getting into the brain.

145
00:11:48,142 --> 00:11:50,896
So predicting that penetration is super important.

146
00:11:51,246 --> 00:11:57,348
Now, for this benchmark, the reigning champion for predicting this was a model called Moleformer.

147
00:11:57,536 --> 00:11:59,536
This is a highly specialized model.

148
00:11:59,630 --> 00:12:04,132
It was trained exclusively on over a billion small molecule sequences.

149
00:12:04,430 --> 00:12:08,656
So think of it as like a hyper-focused master of small molecule chemistry.

150
00:12:08,816 --> 00:12:11,652
On the other hand, this new mammal is a generalist.

151
00:12:11,936 --> 00:12:22,896
But the insane thing is, on this blood brain, barrier penetration test, while the hyper-specialized moleformer achieved a very good score, MAML was able to beat it.

152
00:12:23,118 --> 00:12:27,296
And then same for this Clintox safety benchmark, which predicts FDA approval.

153
00:12:27,470 --> 00:12:30,980
MAML also beat Moleformer by a huge percentage points.

154
00:12:31,296 --> 00:12:34,468
Let's take a second to highlight why this is so impressive.

155
00:12:34,736 --> 00:12:40,176
In almost every field, a specialist will beat a generalist in their specific domain, right?

156
00:12:40,494 --> 00:12:48,132
For example, if a decathlete races a professional swimmer, the decathlete will likely lose, since he's not really specialized in swimming.

157
00:12:48,398 --> 00:12:50,916
Well, in this case, mammal is the decathlete.

158
00:12:51,168 --> 00:12:54,756
It spreads its knowledge across chemistry, genetics, and proteins.

159
00:12:55,088 --> 00:13:01,488
It's not just specialized in small molecule sequences, but yet it was able to beat the specialist pro swimmer.

160
00:13:01,582 --> 00:13:05,476
It beats Moleformer that was highly specialized in chemistry.

161
00:13:05,742 --> 00:13:07,648
That's actually very insightful.

162
00:13:07,822 --> 00:13:11,808
Why does knowing about genes and proteins also make it better at chemistry?

163
00:13:12,014 --> 00:13:15,248
Well, that's because everything is interconnected in biology.

164
00:13:15,438 --> 00:13:25,700
There's a billion moving parts, and this proves that being multimodal, being able to understand all these different domains, is not a distraction but instead an advantage.

165
00:13:26,030 --> 00:13:30,448
Small molecules exist to interact with proteins and alter gene expression.

166
00:13:30,688 --> 00:13:42,020
Because MAML was forced to learn the relationships between all these different modalities during training, it developed a much deeper understanding of the overall biology of the molecules.

167
00:13:42,254 --> 00:13:47,008
And this makes it much better at predicting outcomes compared to a specialized model.

168
00:13:47,214 --> 00:13:49,008
But the researchers didn't stop there.

169
