185
00:15:18,190 --> 00:15:24,288
Next, they tested it on labeling cell types using the Zeng68k dataset.

186
00:15:24,448 --> 00:15:30,528
This consists of thousands of gene activity data from a ton of different immune cell types in the blood.

187
00:15:30,798 --> 00:15:35,728
The AI's job is to look at the genetic activity of the cell and correctly label it.

188
00:15:35,888 --> 00:15:41,088
For example, this is the C D4 plus T cell, or this is the NK cell, etc. etc.

189
00:15:41,328 --> 00:15:44,580
Basically label the cell given its genetic activity.

190
00:15:44,782 --> 00:15:51,620
And this is incredibly important for figuring out how a patient's immune system is responding to a disease or treatment.

191
00:15:51,822 --> 00:15:53,956
It's a fundamental classification task.

192
00:15:54,190 --> 00:16:01,168
And on this highly complex task, MAML achieved a 7.5% improvement over the state-of-the-art model.

193
00:16:01,328 --> 00:16:03,076
Again, this is a huge leap.

194
00:16:03,278 --> 00:16:05,088
And it gets even more impressive.

195
00:16:05,294 --> 00:16:09,316
I think the highlight of this paper is this cancer drug response section.

196
00:16:09,568 --> 00:16:12,676
This is proof of a genuine biomedical breakthrough.

197
00:16:12,878 --> 00:16:14,404
So here's the setup for this test.

198
00:16:14,702 --> 00:16:20,208
The researchers wanted to prove that MAML wasn't just regurgitating information it had memorized from training.

199
00:16:20,368 --> 00:16:27,328
They wanted to see if it could predict how completely new and unseen drugs would perform against human cancer cells.

200
00:16:27,566 --> 00:16:32,608
So they selected four drugs that were definitely not included in its training data.

201
00:16:32,768 --> 00:16:38,848
So this includes carfilzomib, nintidanib, infigratinib, and vemurafenib.

202
00:16:39,182 --> 00:16:40,004
What a mouthful.

203
00:16:40,238 --> 00:16:51,300
Anyway, they took the chemical strings for these four drugs and they gave them to MAML, in addition to the genetic profiles of over 800 different types of cancer tumor cells.

204
00:16:51,598 --> 00:16:55,168
This includes like lung cancer, breast cancer, colon cancer, and so on.

205
00:16:55,408 --> 00:16:57,828
A huge diversity of human cancer cells.

206
00:16:58,094 --> 00:17:05,316
And they asked MAML one question How effective are these four new drugs across these 800 tumor cell types?

207
00:17:05,710 --> 00:17:09,328
Rank them from the most deadly down to the least deadly to the cancer.

208
00:17:09,710 --> 00:17:18,180
Now you might be wondering if these drugs weren't in the training data, what if they still looked very similar to something in the training data that the AI memorized?

209
00:17:18,512 --> 00:17:19,952
That's a very valid concern.

210
00:17:20,206 --> 00:17:30,672
So to prove these drugs were truly new to the model, to prove that it has never seen these types of drugs before, the researchers calculated something called the Tanimoto similarity.

211
00:17:30,832 --> 00:17:37,552
This is basically used to measure how structurally similar two molecules are based on their chemical structure.

212
00:17:37,678 --> 00:17:48,912
And the researchers confirmed that three out of the four drugs had a maximum tanimoto value of less than 0.7 when compared to anything MAML had ever seen.

213
00:17:49,102 --> 00:17:56,832
And in the rigorous world of chemistry, a score below 0.7 means that these drugs are basically structurally different.

214
00:17:57,134 --> 00:18:00,112
So these drugs are like strangers to the AI.

215
00:18:00,272 --> 00:18:04,324
They look completely new to the AI and like nothing it has seen before.

216
00:18:04,750 --> 00:18:12,672
Now back to the task, it needs to rank how potent each drug will be against 805 different variations of cancer tumors.

217
00:18:12,832 --> 00:18:13,952
And here's its prediction.

218
00:18:14,192 --> 00:18:17,712
MAML returned a very confident and specific ranking.

219
00:18:17,872 --> 00:18:22,852
It predicted that carfilzomib is the most potent across the majority of tumors.

220
00:18:23,086 --> 00:18:30,352
It then ranked Nintodanib second, and then this one third, and this one fourth, the least potent of the four.

221
00:18:30,542 --> 00:18:31,780
Now here's the plot twist.

222
00:18:32,112 --> 00:18:40,672
If you actually look up carfilzomib, it's a real FDA-approved drug, but it's currently approved and only used for blood cancers.

223
00:18:40,832 --> 00:18:46,512
All this time, doctors believed that carfilzomib was useless against solid tumors.

224
00:18:46,702 --> 00:18:47,792
They just thought it didn't work.

225
00:18:47,982 --> 00:18:51,620
That's the currently accepted view from expert oncologists.

226
00:18:51,886 --> 00:18:57,764
But here, the AI put this as the number one strongest drug against solid cancer types.

227
00:18:58,032 --> 00:19:00,112
Remember, we don't have this knowledge before.

228
00:19:00,352 --> 00:19:02,276
Experts never thought this would be the case.

229
00:19:02,606 --> 00:19:07,312
If we put this to the test in real life, we would expect it to fail miserably, right?

230
00:19:07,630 --> 00:19:09,392
But here, MAML seems so confident.

231
00:19:09,552 --> 00:19:19,232
It looked at the structure of this drug and confidently said that this would be very strong against solid tumor cells, completely contrary to what doctors think.

232
00:19:19,502 --> 00:19:21,508
So the researchers put this to the test.

233
00:19:21,966 --> 00:19:28,900
They conducted a real life physical experiment where they tested these drugs against solid cancer cells.

234
00:19:29,072 --> 00:19:31,072
And what they found was shocking.

235
00:19:31,310 --> 00:19:34,052
The results perfectly matched MAML's predictions.

236
00:19:34,352 --> 00:19:37,872
It got the precise potency ranking exactly right.

237
00:19:38,286 --> 00:19:44,100
Carfilzomib was the most potent, cascading down to vemurifenib as the least potent.

238
00:19:44,334 --> 00:19:53,392
Mammals successfully preserved this relative order across approximately 95% of all 805 cancer cell types.

239
00:19:53,582 --> 00:19:56,912
Let's take a second to process how insane this is.

240
00:19:57,230 --> 00:20:04,032
An AI that merely reads text strings looked at a blood cancer drug it had never seen before.

241
00:20:04,238 --> 00:20:14,596
It looked at the genetics of hundreds of solid tumors, which is a different thing, and it correctly deduced that this drug would be effective against these solid tumors.

242
00:20:14,926 --> 00:20:18,724
Even though for decades human experts assumed these drugs would not work.

243
00:20:18,992 --> 00:20:21,152
The implications of this are massive.

244
00:20:21,390 --> 00:20:34,752
What we're witnessing is an AI that can generalize to entirely new chemical compounds and accurately predict their uses for treating diseases, which is the entire field of drug discovery, and especially for drug repurposing.

245
00:20:34,912 --> 00:20:42,564
If you're not familiar with drug repurposing, it means taking a drug that already exists and testing whether it can treat a different disease.

246
00:20:42,830 --> 00:20:51,908
Instead of starting from zero and designing a brand new molecule, scientists look at approved drugs or failed drug candidates and ask, could this work somewhere else?

247
00:20:52,112 --> 00:20:59,872
And this is really important because discovering or inventing a new drug from scratch takes like 10 to 15 years and billions of dollars.

248
00:21:00,272 --> 00:21:09,952
But if you have an AI that can scan the entire library of existing drug candidates and see if it can treat another disease, that opens up massive opportunities.

249
00:21:10,192 --> 00:21:16,752
But as incredible as this cancer drug story is, remember that small molecules are just one type of drug.

250
00:21:16,992 --> 00:21:19,072
Another type is the antibody.

251
00:21:19,232 --> 00:21:23,952
These are larger proteins that clamp onto the problematic protein very precisely.

252
00:21:24,302 --> 00:21:27,872
And predicting the structure and application of this is super complicated.

253
00:21:28,046 --> 00:21:33,732
You see, a protein is just a long chain of amino acids that fold into complex 3D shapes.

254
00:21:34,062 --> 00:21:38,192
That shape determines what they stick to and what effect they will have in the body.

255
00:21:38,414 --> 00:21:43,232
But in theory, a protein has a ginormous number of possible ways it could fold.

256
00:21:43,438 --> 00:21:50,112
If it tried every possible shape randomly, it could take longer than the age of the universe to find the correct one.

257
00:21:50,350 --> 00:21:56,420
But in real life, of course, proteins fold almost instantly because physics guide them towards the most stable shape.

258
00:21:56,672 --> 00:22:04,516
So to create a protein-based drug like an antibody, scientists need to understand not just the protein sequence, but how it folds.

259
00:22:04,846 --> 00:22:07,312
How does it move and what does it bind to?

260
00:22:07,566 --> 00:22:13,792
Now, at least in terms of predicting how proteins fold, that's exactly what Google's legendary Alpha Fold does.

261
00:22:14,062 --> 00:22:21,188
But since MAML was also trained on proteins, what happens if we compare MAML with the latest Alpha Fold 3 head to head?

262
00:22:21,486 --> 00:22:23,712
Keep in mind Alpha Fold is super accurate.

263
00:22:23,792 --> 00:22:26,564
It's like the industry standard for predicting proteins.

264
00:22:26,894 --> 00:22:34,020
It's considered like one of the greatest modern scientific achievements, and the team even won a Nobel Prize in Chemistry for this.

265
00:22:34,318 --> 00:22:38,272
Now, we just established that MAML is just a sequence model, right?

266
00:22:38,478 --> 00:22:40,192
It reads text in one dimension.

267
00:22:40,366 --> 00:22:46,212
How can it possibly beat Alpha Fold, which is specialized at predicting the 3D structure of proteins?

268
00:22:46,574 --> 00:22:48,548
Well, the researchers put this to the test.

269
00:22:48,910 --> 00:22:53,700
They showed both MAML and Alpha Fold an antibody and a disease target.

270
00:22:53,902 --> 00:22:56,192
And they asked, will these two stick together?

271
00:22:56,432 --> 00:22:57,392
Yes or no?

272
00:22:57,582 --> 00:23:03,792
They tested both models across seven different well-documented targets, and again, the results were shocking.

273
00:23:04,032 --> 00:23:08,804
MAML actually beat Alpha Fold 3 on five of those seven targets.

274
00:23:09,070 --> 00:23:10,432
Again, this is pretty crazy.

275
00:23:10,670 --> 00:23:18,672
Alpha Fold 3 can literally see 3D shapes of these proteins, so shouldn't it easily see if it fits with the target disease?

276
00:23:18,862 --> 00:23:23,072
How on earth could a one-dimensional text reader like MAML actually win?

277
00:23:23,342 --> 00:23:29,552
To understand why, we have to understand a very flawed assumption about how proteins work at the microscopic level.

278
00:23:29,742 --> 00:23:36,548
You see, in textbooks or in other content, they often describe proteins as just rigid static structures.

279
00:23:36,832 --> 00:23:45,712
Every textbook illustration shows proteins as these solid interlocking shapes, because, well, that's the only way we could historically draw them or photograph them.

280
00:23:45,872 --> 00:23:56,592
But in reality, at least for human proteins, around 30 40% of the sequence consists of intrinsically disordered regions, or IDRs for short.

281
00:23:56,814 --> 00:23:58,340
Now what on earth are these?

282
00:23:58,702 --> 00:24:03,332
These sections of the protein actually don't have a stable 3D shape.

283
00:24:03,552 --> 00:24:06,192
Instead, they're floppy and highly flexible.

284
00:24:06,414 --> 00:24:13,232
An IDR kind of looks and behaves like a piece of wet spaghetti, constantly wiggling, folding, and shifting around.

285
00:24:13,486 --> 00:24:17,284
So actually, protein structures are quite dynamic, not static.

286
00:24:17,472 --> 00:24:23,140
They're not just solid blocks, but they're actually fluid and they can change shapes or move around.

287
00:24:23,342 --> 00:24:38,148
And this is crucial because some of the most important protein targets, like EGFR and HER2, which are notoriously aggressive drivers of breast cancer and stomach cancer, have a ton of these floppy, intrinsically disordered regions.

288
00:24:38,446 --> 00:24:49,872
If you give these floppy regions to Alpha Fold 3, well it struggles to actually predict the structure of this because it's trained on predicting static and solid 3D structures.

289
00:24:50,126 --> 00:24:55,152
Alpha Fold only learned from those frozen snapshots of proteins during training.

290
00:24:55,406 --> 00:25:05,700
So if you give Alpha Fold 3 the sequence of these highly flexible and chaotic targets and ask if a specific antibody will bind to it, it actually does not answer this well.

291
00:25:05,966 --> 00:25:10,500
But if you give the same question to MAML, it had much better predictions.

292
00:25:10,830 --> 00:25:16,752
MAML actually wins on the floppy proteins because it isn't trying to draw a static picture of them.

293
00:25:16,942 --> 00:25:24,512
Because MAML is a large language model, operating on sequences, it doesn't try to force the protein into any specific shape.

294
00:25:24,846 --> 00:25:31,396
Instead, it seems to understand the underlying grammar or the rules of proteins, including floppy parts.

295
00:25:31,822 --> 00:25:35,940
It relies on the math of the sequence and not just the snapshot of the structure.

296
00:25:36,128 --> 00:25:38,288
And this is a brilliant distinction.

297
00:25:38,478 --> 00:25:44,228
It's far more robust at predicting whether an antibody will bind to a disease target.

298
00:25:44,494 --> 00:25:47,684
And again, this unleashes some insane possibilities.

299
00:25:47,918 --> 00:25:54,244
We can now plug all antibody candidates through this AI and ask if it can bind to certain diseases.

300
00:25:54,478 --> 00:25:58,148
And this could lead to a ton of huge discoveries and new drugs.

301
00:25:58,574 --> 00:26:05,088
Now, so far we've only asked MAML questions about existing candidates and whether they respond to certain diseases.

302
00:26:05,294 --> 00:26:09,568
What if we want to generate a completely new drug from scratch?

303
00:26:09,870 --> 00:26:14,116
The crazy thing is they actually designed mammal to be able to do this.

304
00:26:14,446 --> 00:26:18,340
It's not just an analyst, but it can also act as the inventor.

305
00:26:18,606 --> 00:26:24,048
So next the researchers tested its ability to design new antibodies from scratch.

306
00:26:24,270 --> 00:26:28,928
To understand the significance of this, we first need to understand how antibodies work.

307
00:26:29,134 --> 00:26:38,340
An antibody is basically a massive Y-shaped protein used by our immune system to identify and deactivate things like viruses or cancer cells.

308
00:26:38,638 --> 00:26:42,916
Picture it as a giant microscopic claw floating through the bloodstream.

309
00:26:43,278 --> 00:26:55,648
Now, the main body of that Y-shaped claw is quite stable and consistent, but the very tips of the claw, the parts that actually reach out and physically grab the disease antigen, these are highly variable.

310
00:26:55,886 --> 00:27:00,100
These tips are called CDRs or complementarity-determining regions.

311
00:27:00,334 --> 00:27:03,940
And the CDRs are kind of like the fingers at the end of the claw.

312
00:27:04,128 --> 00:27:09,328
These fingers are the most important part of the antibody because they are highly specific.

313
00:27:09,550 --> 00:27:16,768
The sequence of amino acids that coat up these fingers determine exactly what disease the antibody can grab onto.

314
00:27:16,942 --> 00:27:24,932
In fact, the entire field of modern antibody design involves trying to engineer new and better fingers to grab a specific target.

315
00:27:25,358 --> 00:27:27,808
So, how did they set up this test for MAML?

316
00:27:28,208 --> 00:27:33,968
They used a huge dataset called SabDab, which has a ton of different antibodies.

317
00:27:34,158 --> 00:27:42,848
They took thousands of known and successful antibodies, but they artificially erased the CDRs or the fingers, so that the AI couldn't see it.

318
00:27:43,054 --> 00:27:48,868
They then fed MAML the disease it needed to target, and essentially they gave it a fill-in-the-blank test.

319
00:27:49,262 --> 00:27:57,220
Based on this target disease, predict the exact sequence of amino acids the fingers should have to bind onto this disease.

320
00:27:57,582 --> 00:28:01,252
Remember, MAML only reads one-dimensional text sequences.

321
00:28:01,408 --> 00:28:02,848
It's just a language model.

322
00:28:03,008 --> 00:28:07,908
It wasn't allowed to look at any 3D structural maps of the antibody or the disease.

323
00:28:08,208 --> 00:28:15,300
It had to infer the physical shape of both of these just by understanding the grammar or the rules of proteins.

324
00:28:15,662 --> 00:28:16,356
And guess what?

325
00:28:16,558 --> 00:28:18,128
The results were shocking.

326
00:28:18,510 --> 00:28:27,012
MAML was able to generate the correct sequences of all these fingers even better than other state-of-the-art methods that were specialized at this.

327
00:28:27,310 --> 00:28:29,476
But here's where it gets even crazier.

328
00:28:29,648 --> 00:28:36,608
There's one specific region where it completely blew away the competition, and that's the CDRH3 region.

329
00:28:36,814 --> 00:28:39,728
Now what makes this specific finger so special?

330
00:28:39,982 --> 00:28:49,568
You see, this CDRH3 region is notoriously the longest and most complex and chaotic and variable region of the entire antibody.

331
00:28:49,742 --> 00:28:56,768
Because of its length and flexibility, it's almost entirely responsible for determining what the antibody can bind to.

332
00:28:56,942 --> 00:29:01,088
And consequently, it's also the hardest piece for any AI to predict.

333
00:29:01,326 --> 00:29:04,928
And for this region, MAML absolutely crushed the competition.

334
00:29:05,264 --> 00:29:10,852
It achieved a massive 19% improvement compared to the previous leading models.

335
00:29:11,184 --> 00:29:20,100
A 19% leap in predicting the hardest, most variable part of the antibody, using nothing but its underlying understanding of the grammar of proteins.

336
00:29:20,430 --> 00:29:21,664
That's pretty astonishing.

337
00:29:21,902 --> 00:29:27,424
It means the model isn't just memorizing common patterns, it truly understands the rules of biology.

338
00:29:27,630 --> 00:29:30,064
So as we zoom out, what does this all mean?

339
00:29:30,254 --> 00:29:40,260
We potentially have the first true foundation model for biology, one that doesn't just read papers or just look at molecules or analyze genes, but it does all of it at once.

340
00:29:40,590 --> 00:29:43,344
It's one unified model that understands everything.

341
00:29:43,630 --> 00:29:53,984
If MAML actually works the way the paper claims, we could be looking at a future where drug discovery isn't a 10-year billion dollar gamble that fails 90% of the time.

342
00:29:54,144 --> 00:29:59,424
Instead, we can now design drugs way faster and cheaper and more accurately.

343
00:29:59,584 --> 00:30:05,316
We can also explore existing known drug candidates and see if they can target any other disease.

344
00:30:05,582 --> 00:30:10,624
I mean, we can expect a massive acceleration in new drugs in the coming months if this is true.

345
00:30:10,862 --> 00:30:17,024
And because this model also understands DNA and gene expression, personalized medicine could become mainstream.

346
00:30:17,104 --> 00:30:24,932
We can just take a DNA and blood sample from the patient and plug it through this AI to figure out what's causing their specific disease.

347
00:30:25,184 --> 00:30:29,344
What's wrong with their DNA or which proteins in their body are messed up?

348
00:30:29,614 --> 00:30:35,184
And then this AI can also figure out what are the best drugs to prescribe to this specific person.

349
00:30:35,470 --> 00:30:39,424
Or it can even custom design a new antibody just for this patient.

350
00:30:39,726 --> 00:30:47,460
And this is a bold prediction, but in the paper, they've already shown that it's really good at predicting how drug candidates bind to cancer cells.

351
00:30:47,664 --> 00:30:50,532
It already found a new genuine breakthrough.

352
00:30:50,734 --> 00:31:00,864
So, you know, if we keep using this model or if we keep improving this model even further, we could potentially see some new cures for cancer very soon, in addition to other diseases.

353
00:31:01,070 --> 00:31:04,304
This is definitely one of the most impactful papers this year so far.

354
00:31:04,464 --> 00:31:11,344
It's going to have huge impacts across medicine, biosciences, and drug discovery if what they claim is indeed true.

